<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Strandzha Commune</h1><p class="st-meta">1903 CE – 1903 CE · capital Vasiliko</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived insurgent administration in the Strandzha mountains during the Preobrazhenie Uprising of 1903 against Ottoman rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In August 1903, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Adrianople</a> revolutionary district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-macedonian-revolutionary-organization">Internal Macedonian-Adrianople Revolutionary Organisation</a> launched the Preobrazhenie Uprising in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, on the Feast of the Transfiguration. Bulgarian rebels drove Ottoman forces out of much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> mountains, near the Bulgarian border and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the liberated villages the rebels set up a provisional administration, later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> Commune, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsarevo">Vasiliko</a> as a centre. Leaders influenced by anarchist ideas, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihail-gerdzhikov">Mihail Gerdzhikov</a>, organised local self-government, supplies and defence, and promised fair treatment to Greek and Turkish villagers. It lasted only about three weeks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Large Ottoman forces suppressed the uprising by early September 1903, burning villages and driving thousands of refugees into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>, and the commune is remembered as an early experiment in revolutionary self-rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1903 CE – 1903 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.57%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1903 CE – 1903 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vasiliko</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mihail Gerdzhikov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Bulgarian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bulgarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Strandzha Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1903&amp;focus=strandzha-commune" aria-label="Strandzha Commune on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5356 677 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Strandzha Commune lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5676 933L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5676 933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5676 933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1903&amp;focus=strandzha-commune">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
